# November 24, 2026: Seattle, Washington (region wa). The names came from one video about the city's old buildings
# (Old World Exploration, 2024), taken as tips only: every fact below is from a source opened for this batch.
# Eighteen places: Pioneer Square and the old downtown (Smith Tower, the Pioneer Square totem pole, the underground
# sidewalks, the Frye Hotel, the Colman, Hoge and Central buildings, the Burke Building arch, the 1933 Federal Office
# Building, the King County Courthouse, Union Station), the Metropolitan Tract and north downtown (the Cobb Building,
# the Olympic Hotel, the Times Square Building), First Hill (St. James Cathedral) and the University of Washington
# (Suzzallo Library, Parrington Hall, and Architecture Hall from the 1909 world's fair).
# Sources: Wikipedia as raw text and its Seattle Register list; the Pacific Coast Architecture Database (University of
# Washington); SAH Archipedia; the pages of Smith Tower, the Underground Tour and the University of Washington Libraries
# for hours.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
def L(t,u): return [t,u]
BATCH={'key':'seattle_nwbv','label':'Seattle: Pioneer Square, downtown and the university','added':'2026-11-24 00:39'}
DT='Downtown Seattle: street meters and pay garages all round (owners and fees not checked).'
UW='On the Seattle campus of the University of Washington; seen from the campus walks. Parking is in the university\'s pay lots and garages (which to use and the fees not checked).'
PCAD=lambda n,t: L('Pacific Coast Architecture Database: '+t,'https://pcad.lib.washington.edu/building/%d/' % n)
PLACES=[
N('smith-tower-seattle','Smith Tower','Seattle','wa','civic','standing',47.60201,-122.33179,'exact','open','walk','','1911–1914',
  'A 38-storey office tower in Pioneer Square, built by the typewriter and firearms maker Lyman Cornelius Smith and, after his death in 1910, by his son Burns Lyman Smith. It opened on July 4, 1914, when over 4,000 people rode to the 35th floor. It was the tallest building west of the Mississippi until 1931 and the tallest on the West Coast until the Space Needle of 1962. It was a city landmark in 1984.',
  'Granite on the two lowest floors and white terra cotta above, with a pointed top. The 35th-floor observation deck, where the carved teak ceiling and furniture of the old Chinese Room were reused in a bar after 2016, is open to ticket holders; the lobby has Mexican onyx and marble. The ticket price was not checked.',
  'The white tower that was the tallest in the West for 17 years.',
  'Private office building. The observatory and bar are open to the public with a ticket, at the hours on the Smith Tower page. '+DT,
  [W('Smith Tower'),L('Smith Tower: observatory and bar','https://smithtower.com/observatory/')],
  brick='no',hours='Observatory and bar: Monday to Wednesday noon to 7 p.m., Thursday and Sunday noon to 8 p.m., Friday and Saturday noon to 9 p.m.'),
N('pioneer-square-totem-pole-seattle','Pioneer Square totem pole','Seattle','wa','curio','standing',47.60208,-122.33406,'site','public','walk','','1790 (first pole); 1940 (the pole now standing)',
  'A Tlingit pole first carved about 1790 to honour a woman called Chief-of-All-Women, raised in the village on Tongass Island, Alaska. Seattle businessmen took it from the village in 1899 and gave it to the city, which put it up in Pioneer Place. An arsonist damaged it in October 1938; Tlingit carvers led by Charles Brown at Saxman, Alaska, some of them of the family that owned the pole, carved a replica, raised in the square on July 24, 1940. The replica is a National Historic Landmark.',
  'The carved red cedar pole stands in the small triangular plaza at First Avenue and Yesler Way, by the iron pergola and the Pioneer Building.',
  'The pole that gave Seattle a symbol, and how it got there.',
  'The pole belongs to the City of Seattle and stands in Pioneer Place, a small city plaza open at all times. '+DT,
  [W('Pioneer Square totem pole'),W('Pioneer Building (Seattle)')],
  brick='no'),
N('seattle-underground-pioneer-square','Seattle Underground (the old sidewalks under Pioneer Square)','Seattle','wa','civic','ruins',47.60229,-122.33380,'area','open','walk','','1889–1890s',
  'After the Great Fire of June 6, 1889, Seattle rebuilt Pioneer Square in masonry and raised its streets, building retaining walls along the new roadways. The old sidewalks and ground floors were left below, roofed with brick arches and glass vault lights, and for a time people still walked them. Only a small part has been made safe; Bill Speidel\'s Underground Tour has shown it since 1965.',
  'The vault lights, small panes of glass that have turned purple, still show in some sidewalks of the square. Below ground (on the tour only) are the old storefronts and sidewalks under the brick arches.',
  'The city that was buried under its own new streets.',
  'Seen only on the guided tour, about 75 minutes, which starts at 614 First Avenue, beside Pioneer Place; tickets at the counter there or in advance from the tour\'s page. The pin marks where the tour starts; the passages run under several blocks. '+DT,
  [W('Seattle Underground'),L('Bill Speidel\'s Underground Tour','https://www.undergroundtour.com/')],
  brick='some',hours='Every day all year; tours start on the hour from 10 a.m. to 6 p.m., with extra tours on the half hour June to September.'),
N('frye-hotel-seattle','Frye Hotel (Frye Apartments)','Seattle','wa','hotel','standing',47.60146,-122.33069,'exact','view','walk','','1908–1911',
  'An eleven-storey hotel at 223 Yesler Way, built by the pioneer George Frye, who had come to Seattle in 1853, and named for his wife Louisa, a daughter of Arthur and Mary Denny. Bebb and Mendel designed it, and it opened in 1911. The Low Income Housing Institute bought it in 1997 and renovated it; it holds 234 low-income flats.',
  'A tall block with terra-cotta ornament, which was replacing cut stone on Seattle buildings in the 1910s. A home for its tenants: interior not open to visitors.',
  '',
  'Housing owned by the Low Income Housing Institute; look from Yesler Way and Third Avenue. '+DT,
  [PCAD(5347,'Frye Hotel'),W('George Frye')],
  brick='?'),
N('colman-building-seattle','Colman Building','Seattle','wa','civic','standing',47.60348,-122.33549,'exact','view','walk','','1889–1906',
  'An office block on First Avenue between Marion and Columbia streets, built for James Murray Colman, a Scottish machinist who came to Seattle in 1872 and built Colman Dock. Stephen Meany\'s Victorian design of 1888–1889 was rebuilt from 1904 by August Tidemand in a plainer Chicago style. It is on the National Register and a city landmark.',
  'Cast-iron storefront columns and the entrance arch from the first design on the ground floor, rusticated stone on the second, four floors of red brick trimmed with marble above, and a copper cornice. The lobby was rebuilt in 1930 to Arthur Loveless\'s design. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Private offices and shops; look from First Avenue. '+DT,
  [W('Colman Building')],
  brick='yes'),
N('hoge-building-seattle','Hoge Building','Seattle','wa','civic','standing',47.60303,-122.33365,'exact','view','walk','','1911',
  'A 17-storey office tower at 705 Second Avenue, at Cherry Street, built in 1911 by the banker James D. Hoge to designs by Bebb and Mendel, with an eye to earthquakes after San Francisco in 1906. It was the tallest building in Seattle until the Smith Tower of 1914. The lot is said to be where Carson Boren built his cabin, among the first settlers\' houses.',
  'Tan brick and terra cotta over a steel frame, in a Renaissance Revival style with Beaux-Arts touches. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Private offices; look from Second Avenue and Cherry Street. '+DT,
  [W('Hoge Building')],
  brick='no'),
N('burke-building-arch-seattle','Burke Building arch','Seattle','wa','civic','ruins',47.60459,-122.33485,'exact','public','walk','','1889–1891 (building); razed 1971',
  'The six-storey Burke Building stood on the northwest corner of Second Avenue and Marion Street. It was designed by Elmer H. Fisher after the 1889 fire for Judge Thomas Burke, and finished in 1891. It was demolished early in 1971 for the Henry M. Jackson Federal Building, and its entrance arch was kept and set up in front of the new building.',
  'The arch of the main entry, trimmed in rusticated stone, stands alone on the plaza in front of the federal building that replaced it. A plaque there says the Burke Building was built on the site in 1895, where the architecture database gives 1889–1891.',
  'One doorway left of a building of the fire rebuild.',
  'On the plaza of the Henry M. Jackson Federal Building, seen from the Second Avenue sidewalk. '+DT,
  [PCAD(4656,'Burke Building'),W('Henry M. Jackson Federal Building')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('federal-office-building-seattle','Federal Office Building','Seattle','wa','civic','standing',47.60410,-122.33671,'exact','view','walk','','1930–1933',
  'An early federal building in the Art Deco style, at 909 First Avenue, designed in 1930–1931 in the office of the Supervising Architect under James A. Wetmore. Congress had approved over $2 million for it in 1928. The Great Fire of 1889 began in a cabinet shop on this block, and the building\'s cornerstone and two plaques for the fire are near where it started.',
  'Granite base, a first storey of smooth terra cotta, light red brick above, and pale terra-cotta Art Deco ornament with small ram and lion heads at the entrances; cast aluminium panels between the windows. Inside, a lobby of Wilkeson sandstone and a post-office lobby nearly as built, with oak service windows. When the public lobby and post office are open was not checked.',
  'The block where the 1889 fire started.',
  'A federal office building with a post office. Look from First Avenue; hours of the post-office lobby not checked. '+DT,
  [W('Federal Office Building (Seattle)')],
  brick='yes'),
N('central-building-seattle','Central Building','Seattle','wa','civic','standing',47.60475,-122.33281,'exact','view','walk','','1907',
  'An office building at 810 Third Avenue, designed by Charles Ronald Aldrich after the San Francisco earthquake of 1906 and finished in 1907. The first meetings of the Port of Seattle commission were held here in 1911. It was renovated from 2002 to 2007 and made a city landmark in 2008.',
  'Its front looks like granite but is terra cotta. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Private offices; look from Third Avenue. '+DT,
  [W('Central Building (Seattle)')],
  brick='?'),
N('king-county-courthouse-seattle','King County Courthouse','Seattle','wa','civic','standing',47.60264,-122.33053,'exact','view','walk','','1914–1916; raised 1930',
  'Seattle and King County\'s City-County Building, at 516 Third Avenue on land once owned by Henry Yesler. A. Warren Gould proposed a 23-storey tower, but the county built five storeys, dedicated on May 4, 1916; six more were added in 1930. It still holds the county\'s courts, prosecutor, sheriff and council, and was made a county landmark in 1987, which limits changes to the public rooms.',
  'A long block facing City Hall Park. Interior not checked; a working courthouse, where entry rules and screening were not checked.',
  '',
  'King County\'s courthouse; look from Third Avenue and City Hall Park. '+DT,
  [W('King County Courthouse')],
  brick='?'),
N('union-station-seattle','Union Station','Seattle','wa','rail','standing',47.59867,-122.32854,'exact','view','walk','','1910–1911',
  'The station of the Union Pacific and the Milwaukee Road, at Fourth Avenue South and South Jackson Street; it opened on May 20, 1911 as Oregon and Washington Station. The Milwaukee Road left in 1961 and the Union Pacific in 1971, and the building stood nearly empty for almost 30 years. A renovation backed by Paul Allen won a national preservation award in 2000, and it has been the head office of Sound Transit since 1999.',
  'The great hall, used for weddings and events. When it may be walked into on other days was not checked: interior not checked.',
  'A station with no trains, saved by a software fortune.',
  'Sound Transit\'s offices; look from South Jackson Street and Fourth Avenue South. '+DT,
  [W('Union Station (Seattle)')],
  brick='?'),
N('cobb-building-seattle','Cobb Building','Seattle','wa','civic','standing',47.60832,-122.33537,'exact','view','walk','','1909–1910',
  'An eleven-storey building at 1301 Fourth Avenue, at University Street, built in 1909–1910 to designs by Howells & Stokes, an early high-rise for doctors\' offices. It was the third building on the Metropolitan Tract, the university\'s old downtown land, and is the only one left of the buildings of its design that lined Fourth Avenue. It is now flats.',
  'Terra-cotta heads of Native Americans at the cornice of the ninth and tenth floors, credited to Victor G. Schneider. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Private flats; look from Fourth Avenue and University Street. '+DT,
  [W('Cobb Building (Seattle)')],
  brick='?'),
N('olympic-hotel-seattle','Olympic Hotel (Fairmont Olympic)','Seattle','wa','hotel','standing',47.60806,-122.33393,'exact','open','walk','','1922–1924',
  'Seattle\'s grand hotel, on the Metropolitan Tract where the university first stood. George B. Post & Son of New York designed it in an Italian Renaissance style, with Bebb and Gould as local architects, and it opened on December 6, 1924. It was listed on the National Register in 1979, renovated by Four Seasons in the early 1980s, and again from 2020 to 2022.',
  'A working hotel; interior not checked.',
  '',
  'A working hotel at 411 University Street; hours of its public rooms not checked. '+DT,
  [W('Fairmont Olympic Hotel')],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
N('times-square-building-seattle','Times Square Building','Seattle','wa','civic','standing',47.61251,-122.33816,'exact','view','walk','','1916',
  'A five-storey flatiron of 1916, at 414 Olive Way, surrounded by Fourth and Fifth avenues, Olive Way and Stewart Street. Bebb and Gould designed it for the Seattle Times, which was edited here until 1930. It was listed on the National Register in 1983 and made a city landmark in 1984.',
  'Beaux-Arts front on a wedge-shaped block. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Private; look from the sidewalks round it. '+DT,
  [W('Times Square Building')],
  brick='?'),
N('st-james-cathedral-seattle','St. James Cathedral','Seattle','wa','worship','standing',47.60768,-122.32579,'exact','view','walk','','1905–1907',
  'The Catholic cathedral of the Archdiocese of Seattle, at 804 Ninth Avenue on First Hill. The cornerstone was laid on November 12, 1905 and it was dedicated in 1907. On February 2, 1916 its 60-foot dome fell in under heavy snow; it was never rebuilt, and the church reopened in March 1917 with its inside much changed. It was made a city landmark in 1984.',
  'Stained glass by Charles Connick of 1917–1920, three windows of 1994 by Hans Gottfried von Stockhausen, an oculus over the altar where the dome stood, an organ by Hutchings-Votey in the west gallery and one by Casavant of 1926, and an altarpiece by Neri di Bicci of 1456 in the chapel. When it is open and its Mass times were not checked: its own page could not be read when this was written.',
  'The dome that fell in the snow of 1916.',
  'An active cathedral; times of services and opening not checked. '+DT,
  [W('St. James Cathedral (Seattle)'),L('St. James Cathedral','https://www.stjames-cathedral.org/')],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
N('suzzallo-library-seattle','Suzzallo Library','Seattle','wa','civic','standing',47.65581,-122.30805,'exact','open','walk','','1923–1926; 1935',
  'The main library of the University of Washington, in Collegiate Gothic by Bebb and Gould. The west wing, facing Red Square, was finished in 1926, the south wing in 1935, and a third wing in 1963 in a modern style. The planned 300-foot bell tower was never built. It is named for Henry Suzzallo, the university\'s president until 1926.',
  'Terra-cotta figures of thinkers by Allen Clark on the front, stone coats of arms of universities, and three cast-stone figures over the entrance. Inside, the Graduate Reading Room, 250 feet long under a painted timber vault, with oak bookcases carved with native plants and tall leaded windows.',
  'A reading room built as a cathedral of learning.',
  UW+' The library page gives its hours; they change with the university term.',
  [W('Suzzallo Library'),L('University of Washington Libraries: Suzzallo and Allen','https://www.lib.washington.edu/suzzallo')],
  brick='?',hours='As read for the week of November 24, 2026: Monday and Tuesday 8 a.m. to 8 p.m., Saturday closed, Sunday 1 to 8 p.m.; hours change with the term'),
N('parrington-hall-seattle','Parrington Hall (Science Hall)','Seattle','wa','civic','standing',47.65744,-122.31025,'exact','view','walk','','1901–1902',
  'Built in 1901–1902 to designs by Josenhans and Allen as Science Hall, for geology, botany and zoology, with the state museum in a rear wing. It was the sixth building on the campus at its present site. It was renamed in 1931 for the English professor Vernon L. Parrington, winner of the 1928 Pulitzer Prize in history, and rehabilitated in 1989.',
  'Three storeys in a Romanesque Revival style, with half-round wings east and west, arched windows and big gable roofs, of red brick and Tenino sandstone from the state. The brick, painted in 1909, was cleaned back in 1989. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  UW+' Seen from the campus walks just north of Red Square.',
  [L('SAH Archipedia: Parrington Hall','https://sah-archipedia.org/node/79319')],
  brick='yes'),
N('architecture-hall-seattle','Architecture Hall (the 1909 Fine Arts Building)','Seattle','wa','civic','standing',47.65456,-122.31096,'exact','view','walk','','1908–1909',
  'Built for the Alaska–Yukon–Pacific Exposition of 1909 as its Fine Arts Building, to a design by John Galen Howard of Howard and Galloway, the fair\'s supervising architects, and paid for by the state to serve the university afterwards. It was the chemistry building until 1937 and has housed architecture since the 1950s, named Architecture Hall in 1957. It is the only one of the fair\'s permanent buildings still on its first site.',
  'Two storeys of cream brick with terra-cotta trim, four Ionic columns at the east entrance and Ionic pilasters along the sides; inside, the first central stair. Interior not checked.',
  'What is left of Seattle\'s world\'s fair.',
  UW+' Seen from West Stevens Way.',
  [L('SAH Archipedia: Architecture Hall','https://sah-archipedia.org/node/79317'),PCAD(4099,'Architecture Hall'),W('Alaska–Yukon–Pacific Exposition')],
  brick='no'),
]
